# Whether a ball with a one-limb center contains another, Y inside X (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", the row of contains(Y), "Exact pairs"): |c_y - c_x| <= r_x - r_y, decided exactly as
# le_2_2(abs_2(two_sum_ord(c_y, -c_x)), two_sum_ord(r_x, -r_y)). A difference of the centers that
# overflows exceeds every radius. The special states, by the table of "The empty set and the whole
# line": the whole line contains every ball, and the empty set is in every ball; the comparison
# gives false for an empty X and a nonempty Y, and for a whole line Y in a finite X (r_x - inf is
# -inf). Exact.
kernel:        ball_contains_ball_1
input:         stored cx
input:         stored rx
input:         stored cy
input:         stored ry
output:        mask m
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       certain m subset y x x=cx,rx y=cy,ry exact
min_precision: 2
source:        the distance of the centers and the difference of the radii as exact pairs, compared (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeBallPredicates.v ball_contains_ball_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the results are masks, which have no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical ball

ncx      = neg(cx)
(d0, d1) = two_sum_ord(cy, ncx)
(a0, a1) = abs_2(d0, d1)
nry      = neg(ry)
(g0, g1) = two_sum_ord(rx, nry)
f        = le_2_2(a0, a1, g0, g1)
wx       = eq(rx, const inf)
qy       = eq(ry, ry)
ey       = not(qy)
sp       = or(wx, ey)
m        = or(sp, f)
